# DESCRIPTION

Each Ethernet device is a directory, as Plan 9's ether(3) has it: a driver of the `net` class serves it, and devmgr(8) binds the first at `/net/ether0`, the next at `/net/ether1`, and so on.

The device has eight conversations, numbered from 0, each a directory of its own made the first time it is used.
Opening `clone` takes the first conversation not in use and gives a channel to its `ctl`; reading that channel gives the conversation's number.
A conversation stays in use while its `ctl` or `data` is open, and when the last of those closes it is reset: its type 0, not promiscuous, its frames dropped.

`ctl` takes these commands, one a write:

: `connect` <type>
  Take frames of Ethernet type <type>, a number in C's notation (`0x800` for IPv4, `0x806` for ARP, `0x86dd` for IPv6).
  A positive type another conversation has connected to is refused.
  `-1` takes frames of every type; `-2` takes every frame as a header record (below), with no length or time.
: `promiscuous`
  Take frames sent to other hosts too.
: `headersonly`
  Take, for each frame, a 64-byte record: the frame's first 64 bytes, its length in bytes 58 and 59 and the milliseconds since boot in bytes 60 to 63, big-endian.
: `addmulti` <address>, `delmulti` <address>, `remmulti` <address>
  Take, or stop taking, frames sent to the multicast <address>, 12 hex digits with or without colons.
: `nonblocking`
  Accepted; writes never wait.

Any other command is refused.
Reading `ctl` gives the conversation's number, as `type` gives its type: right-justified in 11 columns and followed by a blank, as a 32-bit unsigned number (so `-1` reads `4294967295`).

Each read of `data` returns one frame, the 14-byte header (destination, source, type) and its payload, without the checksum; a read shorter than the frame gets its start and loses the rest.
A read waits until a frame comes.
A conversation holds up to 64 frames; frames past that are dropped and counted as soft overflows.
A conversation takes the frames of its type sent to the device's address, to the broadcast address, or to a multicast address it added, and, if promiscuous, every frame of its type.

Each write to `data` is one frame, header included, of 60 to 1514 bytes; the source address in it is replaced by the device's.
A frame written to the broadcast or a multicast address is also given to the device's conversations of its type, as one received would be.

`addr` reads as the device's address, 12 hex digits.
`stats`, in the device's directory and in each conversation's, reads as Plan 9's lines:

```text
in: 4
link: 1
out: 2
crc errs: 0
overflows: 0
soft overflows: 0
framing errs: 0
buffer errs: 0
output errs: 0
prom: 0
mbps: 1000
addr: 525400123456
```

`in` and `out` count frames; `overflows` counts frames the device lost for want of a buffer, `output errs` frames the driver couldn't send, `prom` the promiscuous conversations.
`ifstats` is the driver's own text.

`link` reads as `up` or `down`, a line, as the driver last saw the device's link.
A channel's first read returns the state at once; each read after waits until the state differs from what that channel last read, so a reader hears every change, and ip(4) hears the link come up.
Plan 9's ether(3) has no `link`; its `stats` say `link:` and nothing waits on it.

